#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124156 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "225b0bca-3520-58ab-bd36-0e3de337b604" :question/math-academy-id "e-3068" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y)$. The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2$. Given that the point $P(2,1,8)$ lies on $C$, and that $f_y = \\dfrac{4x}y - 4x^2y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2$. So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,1,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_y(2,1)$.\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ at $(2,1)$:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}{f}_{y} ( 2 , 1 ) & = \\frac{4 ( 2 )}{( 1 )} - 4 ( 2 {)}^{2} ( 1 ) \\\\  & = 8 - 16 \\\\  & = - 8\\end{array}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124157 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "70bcf628-cf1f-5da0-85b7-19d0f514ccd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3069" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S$, defined by $z=f(x,y)$, with the plane $y=\\pi$. Given that the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that $f_x = y\\cos(xy) + \\cos y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z = f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi$. So, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x} \\Bigg| _{(1,\\pi)}\n$$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x}\n$$\n\nat $(1,\\pi)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} \\mid _{(1, \\pi )} &= \\pi \\cos (1 \\cdot \\pi ) + \\cos \\pi  \\\\\n&= \\pi (-1) + (-1) \\\\\n&= - \\pi  - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz - z \\mid _{(1, \\pi )} &= \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} \\mid _{(1, \\pi )}(x - 1) \\\\\nz - (-1) &= (- \\pi  - 1)(x - 1) \\\\\nz + 1 &= (- \\pi  - 1)x + \\pi  + 1 \\\\\nz &= - (\\pi  + 1)x + \\pi \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ has the equation $y=\\pi$.\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = \\pi \\\\ z = - ( \\pi  + 1 ) x + \\pi\\end{array} \\right.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124158 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "75c0deaf-38a7-5a81-afac-cae2978d52b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3070" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S$, defined by $z=f(x,y)$, with the plane $y=1$. Given that the point $P(0,1,1)$ lies on $C$, and that $f_x = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi}{4} \\sin{\\left(2 x + \\dfrac{\\pi y}{4} \\right)}$, find a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1$. So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_x(0,1)$.\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_x(x,y)$ at $(0,1)$:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}{f}_{x} ( 0 , 1 ) & = \\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi}{4} \\sin   ( 2 ( 0 ) + \\frac{\\pi  ( 1 )}{4} ) \\\\  & = \\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi}{4} \\sin   ( \\frac{\\pi }{4} ) \\\\  & = \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{array}$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz - f(0, 1) &= f_{x}(0, 1)(x - 0) \\\\\nz - 1 &= \\frac{\\pi }{4}x \\\\\nz &= \\frac{\\pi }{4}x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(0,1,1)$ has the equation $y=1$.\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 1 \\\\ z = \\frac{\\pi }{4} x + 1\\end{array} \\right.$$\n\nTo find a tangent vector, we can find a normal vector to each plane and compute their cross product.\n\n- For the plane $y = 1$, we write $0x + 1y + 0z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 0,1,0 \\rangle$.\n- For the plane $z = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x +1$, we write $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x + 0y + 1z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{\\pi}4,0,1 \\right\\rangle$.\n\nComputing the cross product, we get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}{\\mathbf{n}}_{1} \\times  {\\mathbf{n}}_{2} & = \\langle  0 , 1 , 0 \\rangle  \\times  \\langle  - \\frac{\\pi }{4} , 0 , 1 \\rangle \\\\  & = \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ - \\pi  / 4 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\\\  & = \\mathbf{i} + \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\, \\mathbf{k} .\\end{array}$$\n\nAt this point, we've found a tangent vector, and this could be our final answer. However, just to make our answer a little nicer, we can multiply the vector by $4$ to yield a simpler, parallel vector:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{r}4 \\cdot  ( \\mathbf{i} + \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\, \\mathbf{k} ) = 4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\pi  \\, \\mathbf{k}\\end{array}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bde33115-b5ec-568b-b700-2ecc5bb38784" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122261} {:answer/id #uuid "d12b216a-9e2e-57e3-9454-23c33fd456d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122262} {:answer/id #uuid "1ff395c8-9356-503b-a7b9-b06acd6e991b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122263} {:answer/id #uuid "39e67ac9-e3c4-53db-a615-f95a9f55b58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122264} {:answer/id #uuid "60c44c1e-bf6a-54fa-a439-0cc39843ba68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60c44c1e-bf6a-54fa-a439-0cc39843ba68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122265} :answer-field/id #uuid "e784d29d-4fb8-57f1-a536-c279710d5670" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122260}] :question/id #uuid "63eca657-711b-57df-bd85-bce2f11b802d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36216" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z = f(x, y)$. The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y = 3$. Given that the point $P(2, 3, 9)$ lies on $C$, and that $f_{x} = 6x - y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a7b3185-4e40-5760-b6eb-4963ed6d1665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 2, 0,-3\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122381} {:answer/id #uuid "a3811862-f2a7-5d8c-a023-a86f1c1db32c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle  - 1, 1, 2\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122382} {:answer/id #uuid "150a3107-545d-53b2-b09f-026482bd47fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 2, 0, 3\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122383} {:answer/id #uuid "17d04bbe-bc43-5954-906c-d713281688f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 1, 1,-2\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122384} {:answer/id #uuid "667e309b-b027-563f-8876-e4d7303d6c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 1, 0, 6\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "667e309b-b027-563f-8876-e4d7303d6c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 1, 0, 6\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122385} :answer-field/id #uuid "907d93d2-7a32-5f23-ade8-14eeeb47988c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122380}] :question/id #uuid "38a2ac51-d5be-54ed-b565-78bd1a3a67e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-36371" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S$, defined by $z = f(x, y)$, with the plane $y =-3$. Given that the point $P(2,-3, 3)$ lies on $C$, and that $f_{x} = 6(x - 1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$?"}]}
